      "title": "Room-temperature entanglement made to last 240 times longer",
      "summary": "Researchers led by USTC's Shuo Ren and Rui-Jian Liang transferred entangled states from electron spins in solid-state defects to nearby atomic nuclei, which resist noise far better. This nuclear-spin swap extended entanglement lifetime at room temperature by up to 240 times. The result points toward sturdier room-temperature quantum memories, but it remains a single-defect lab demonstration far from a usable device.",
